# @PROJECT_NAME@

Generated Qornix Web application with Schema-driven Dynamic API backend and an Angular frontend scaffold.

This template keeps the existing `qornix_dynamic_api` backend behavior and adds a normal Angular application on top of it. The generated backend is ready for frontend development: it exposes Dynamic API endpoints, OpenAPI JSON and the legacy backend/debug pages, while the Angular app is served at `/` in production.

## What this template gives you

- Ready C++ backend linked to `qornix::web_core` and `qornix::dynamic_api`.
- Schema-driven Dynamic API under `/api/dynamic/*`.
- Existing backend/admin/debug pages moved under `/backend/*`.
- Angular frontend scaffold under `frontend/`.
- Angular home page at `/`.
- Angular backend hub at `/backend-admin`.
- Angular project structure page at `/project-structure` with archive download.
- Frontend production build copied into the backend deploy bundle during `cmake --build .`.
- Clear extension points for custom high-performance C++ backend handlers and routes.

The generated backend is a normal Qornix Web application, not a closed generated artifact. Use the Dynamic API for standard data access, then add your own C++ handlers and routes when the product needs custom operations, integrations, reports, imports or performance-sensitive business logic.

The XML-driven UI constructor is intentionally not implemented in this first scaffold. Projects that need it can build that feature inside the Angular app later.

## Project layout

```text
@PROJECT_NAME@/
├── CMakeLists.txt
├── README.md
├── runtime-Dockerfile
├── backend/
│   ├── main.cpp
│   ├── routes.h
│   ├── config.yaml
│   ├── handlers/
│   ├── templates/
│   ├── static/
│   ├── schema/
│   └── doc/
└── frontend/
    ├── angular.json
    ├── package.json
    ├── proxy.conf.json
    ├── tsconfig.json
    ├── tsconfig.app.json
    └── src/
```

## Backend build and run

```bash
mkdir -p build
cd build
cmake ..
cmake --build .
```

The build creates the executable and a portable deploy bundle:

```text
build/@PROJECT_NAME@
build/deploy/@PROJECT_NAME@/
```

Run from the deploy bundle:

```bash
cd build/deploy/@PROJECT_NAME@
./@PROJECT_NAME@
```

Open:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8008/
http://127.0.0.1:8008/backend-admin
http://127.0.0.1:8008/project-structure
```

## Frontend development

Start the backend first, then use the Angular dev server in `frontend/`:

```bash
cd frontend
npm install
npm run dev
```

The dev server proxies backend paths to `http://127.0.0.1:8008`:

```text
/api/*
/backend/*
/static/*
/health
```

Frontend code can use relative paths such as:

```ts
fetch('/api/dynamic/meta/tables')
```

## Production frontend build

The normal CMake build also builds the Angular frontend:

```text
cmake --build .
```

CMake runs these commands in `frontend/` with Angular CLI analytics disabled for non-interactive builds:

```bash
NG_CLI_ANALYTICS=false npm install
NG_CLI_ANALYTICS=false npm run build
```

Then it copies `frontend/dist` into the deploy bundle as `frontend/`.

`npm` is required for this template. If `npm` is not available, CMake fails with a clear error.

## Deploy bundle layout

```text
build/deploy/@PROJECT_NAME@/
├── @PROJECT_NAME@
├── config.yaml
├── README.md
├── backend/
│   ├── templates/
│   ├── static/
│   ├── schema/
│   └── doc/
├── frontend/
│   ├── index.html
│   └── assets/
└── logs/
```

The app discovers its root at runtime from `QORNIX_APP_ROOT`, the executable location, the current working directory and the compile-time source directory fallback. For normal use, run from `build/deploy/@PROJECT_NAME@`.

## Routes

```text
GET /                              Angular home page
GET /backend-admin                 Angular backend hub page
GET /project-structure             Angular project structure browser and archive download page
GET /assets/{filename}             Angular build assets when present
GET /{filename}                    Angular root build files such as main/style chunks
GET /backend                       legacy Dynamic API landing page
GET /backend/schema-manager        Schema Manager UI
GET /backend/query-builder         visual structured query builder
GET /backend/table                 table browser
GET /backend/table/{table}/{id}    row view with inline update/delete
GET /backend/api-playground        Dynamic API recipes
GET /backend/docs                  lightweight OpenAPI viewer
GET /backend/docs/schema           schema guide
GET /backend/docs/query-syntax     query syntax guide
GET /backend/instructions          generated app quick start
GET /static/{filename}             legacy backend page assets
GET /health                        JSON health endpoint
GET /api/project/structure         JSON project directory listing for /project-structure
GET /api/project/archive           compressed project snapshot download
GET /api/dynamic/openapi.json      raw OpenAPI document
GET /api/dynamic/meta/tables       Dynamic API metadata
```


## Extending the backend

The generated backend is built on Qornix Web:

```text
https://github.com/hairetdin/qornix_web
```

Use the generated Dynamic API first for CRUD, metadata, query and OpenAPI-driven frontend work. When generic endpoints are not enough, add custom C++ logic in the backend application:

```text
backend/handlers/      reusable request handlers and business operations
backend/routes.h       route registration for additional endpoints
/api/operations/*      recommended namespace for custom product actions
```

Good candidates for custom C++ handlers:

- domain operations that are not simple CRUD;
- imports, exports and report generation;
- integrations with external services;
- validation workflows and business rules;
- performance-sensitive backend logic.

This keeps the frontend simple: Angular calls generated endpoints for normal data access and calls custom backend endpoints only for product-specific workflows.

## Useful frontend documentation

- Angular docs: https://angular.dev/overview
- Angular CLI: https://angular.dev/tools/cli
- Angular routing: https://angular.dev/guide/routing
- Angular HTTP client: https://angular.dev/guide/http
- RxJS guide: https://rxjs.dev/guide/overview

## Recommended first run

1. Open `/backend-admin` to see quick start commands and useful backend links.
2. Open `/project-structure` to inspect the generated layout and download a project snapshot.
3. Open `/backend/schema-manager`.
4. Click `Load demo schema`, then run `Validate -> Diff -> Plan -> Apply live plan`.
5. Open `/backend/table`, `/backend/query-builder` or `/backend/api-playground`.
6. Start editing the Angular app in `frontend/src`.

## Runtime Docker image

Build the app first:

```bash
mkdir -p build
cmake -S . -B build
cmake --build build --target @PROJECT_NAME@_deploy
docker build -f runtime-Dockerfile -t @PROJECT_NAME@:runtime .
```

Run:

```bash
docker run --rm -p 8008:8008 @PROJECT_NAME@:runtime
```

The image stores application files in `/app`, data in `/data` and file logs in `/logs`. For persistent SQLite data and logs, use named volumes:

```bash
docker volume create @PROJECT_NAME@_data
docker volume create @PROJECT_NAME@_logs
docker run -d --name @PROJECT_NAME@ \
  -p 8008:8008 \
  -v @PROJECT_NAME@_data:/data \
  -v @PROJECT_NAME@_logs:/logs \
  @PROJECT_NAME@:runtime
```

## Future direction

A later project-specific iteration can turn this scaffold into an XML-driven frontend constructor:

- UI XML schema files.
- Dynamic navigation.
- Generated list/detail pages.
- Relation tabs.
- Editable forms.
- Backend operation buttons.

That work remains optional because many projects only need a conventional Angular frontend over the generated backend API.
